      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Kickapoo people began as an Algonquian‑speaking Woodland tribe in the Great Lakes region, farming corn, beans, and squash while hunting and gathering.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/kickapoo_site_6_kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The area has a humid continental climate, averaging 53.3°F annually, with hot summers and brief, severe winter cold spells.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/kickapoo_site_6_kansas",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Tallgrass prairie once dominated the landscape, but much has become cropland; remaining riparian zones and wetlands support diverse plant communities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/kickapoo_site_6_kansas",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The name Kickapoo comes from words meaning 'he stands about' or 'he moves about standing here and there,' hinting at a semi‑nomadic past.",
